From: Claude QA <qa@example.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 23:03:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Client onboarding: the backfill walks jobs by rowid with a
 cursor instead of re-reading every active job on every call

POST /api/clients/:id/backfill selected jobs ORDER BY discovered_at with NOT EXISTS filters. The planner read the is_active index and sorted EVERY active job on every call,
so a call cost the whole catalogue however far onboarding had got. Measured locally (counting job rows that reach the WHERE clause, 21,120 active jobs): 11,151,360 job rows
scanned across 528 calls (21,120 per call); at about 4 rows read per scanned job (index entry, table row, two NOT EXISTS probes) that is about 44.6 million rows read against the
5 million daily free allowance. The walk is now by rowid, newest first, from a cursor (next_cursor, returned by the previous call): 21,647 job rows scanned in total, 515 times
fewer. Callers without a cursor still work (they skip screened jobs from the top). The dashboard's 'screen all' passes the cursor along.
Tests: cursor walk screens each active job once; cursor and no-cursor end identically; newest first; COST (the real function with the job-row loop instrumented: <= 81 per call,
about N in total; the old code visits 1,000 per call and fails the bound 23x); query plan has no sort and no scan; route accepts and sanitises the cursor.

